ORIGIN:
=======

  I found out while setting up C-Net one time that the quote wall program
  that came with a disk I got really sucked the big one. So, I downloaded
  and tried a few more, they all stunk like yesterday's abortions. I did
  not like how ANY of them worked. So, I set out to mangle my own in
  ARexx. Thus becometh, the birtheth ofeth T-QUOTE(eth). It started out
  to be extremly buggy and tiny, yet it sorta worked...

  After a few months of minor thingies.. I got to version 4.36 I think,
  and it still sucked! So I re-wrote the entire thing! Thus I came up
  TQuote5.0, highly functional, very fast. It worked great, but I then
  came up with the idea "More options, the better!". I added in several
  new things, and fixed them, etc. I got to 5.5x and I needed to do a
  overhaul of how it worked. It was pretty hosed up! I came out with
  version 6.0 on July 30th, 1992. This was a major step for the well
  being of TQuote. Now, as you read on, the POWER of TQuote is lingering
  somewhere on your computer, waiting to spring!! Now we are at version
  7.0a! Yipee!!!

INSTALLATION:
=============

  In this ARCHIVE there should be:

  File:                      Size:
  Text.quotes                 2371 - Sample Cnet 2.xx Text Data file
  TQuote7a                   18188 - The Cnet 2.xx Version of TQuote
  BlueSilver.displayme        1331 - My board's AD!
  TQuote7a.Doc                9389 - This file, ummm how can you
                                     read this if it's not here?

  * Due to many requests, I no longer include the Cnet 1.xx versions
    of TQuote. If you are too sorry and pitiful to not send in your
    fees, then what business do you have calling Future World?

  * Due to many requests, the ZZZEndPad.foo is no longer needed. It
    has been written directly into TQuote.

  If ANY of these files are missing, or the SIZES are different, you
  might be running a mangled version of TQuote7a. If this is so, please
  call the board you downloaded it from and piss and moan to the Sysop
  that you WANT THE REAL ARCHIVE dammit! If the Sysop gets pissed cuz
  you yelled at him and boots you off, call my board and you will have
  access. No one on my board is denied access! Everyone is validated
  (unless they are some damn clone who has no life!). I am a nice guy.

LETS REALLY INSTALL THIS THING DAMMIT!!
=======================================

  All ya do, is make a directory in SYSDATA: called Quotes. Then, copy
  the Text.quotes file to SYSDATA:Quotes/ directory. This should be
  simple enough. Now, copy TQuote7a to the PFILES: directory (or wherever
  you wanna put it). Now you are all set up to go! Add it is a PFile that
  that more than one person can use, and set yer access to what it should
  be for your system, or you can add it as a menu command from the main
  menu:

    (Add this line to your BBSMENU file as the LAST item under Available
     everywhere.)

    TQuote | \#0PFILES:<path/file>\

    (path/file ex: \#0PFILES:Utils/TQuote7a\ )

    Or, you can simply add it at log on in your Sys.Welcome file! Like
    this:

    \#0PFILES:<path/file>\

  Now, before you run this program first, you should go and configure
  the Text.quotes file, and the TQuote7a file to change the system variables
  to what you want. These will be discussed now.

bortex='\c2'    /* Colour for text INSIDE border.                          */
timtex='\c4'    /* Colour for the quote's time                             */
hantex='\c2'    /* Colour for the quote's HANDLE                           */
lintex='\c7'    /* The colour for the >----< thingy in a quote.            */
siz='10'                   /* Min. size of quote in order for saving.      */
as=1                       /* Ask users if they wanna read it? 0=No, 1=Yes */
cz=1                       /* Search for naughty words! 0=No, 1=Yes        */
qn=5                       /* Maximum number of quotes (9999999999 max)    */
cm=1                       /* Cute mode 1=on, 0=off - Great fun!           */
dt='SYSDATA:Quotes/Sys.QuoteNum.' /* Quote Data File, must be in own Dir!! */
dd='SYSDATA:Quotes'               /* Quote Directory (Same as above)       */
txd='SYSDATA:Quotes/Text.Quotes'  /* Name of TEXT data file                */

  These should be pretty well self explanatory, if not, ask a local C-Net
  jockey. He's bound to know.

